<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_tabletop_tee">
  <!-- The table, T-block and goal shared by open_table, single_obstacle,
       shelf_gap and ycb_clutter. Those four IsaacGym tasks (sim_task01..04)
       differ *only* in their obstacle field: the same block starts at the
       same pose and is pushed to the same goal in all four, so all of that
       lives here once and each scene file adds only its own obstacles.

       The camera is *not* included here: shelf_gap needs a steeper
       elevation than the other three to see over its shelves (its own
       comment has the derivation), so each scene declares its own
       `<camera name="front">`.

       RE-SCALED TO THE LAB (2026-08-25). This family used to be IsaacGym's
       own metric world -- a 1.40 x 2.50 m table and a 0.200 x 0.150 m T --
       which meant every sim result had to be re-derived before it could be
       trusted on hardware. The table, the block and its physics are now the
       measured lab ones, verbatim from ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop_real/, so a
       plan made here is a plan the real cell can execute:

           table   1.40 x 2.50 x 0.14 m  ->  0.800 x 1.523 x 0.910 m
           T plan  0.200 x 0.150 m       ->  0.089 x 0.099 m
           mass    2.0 kg                ->  0.1 kg
           mu      0.4                   ->  0.3

       The table is 3.7 cm DEEPER than the lab's own 0.763 m (2026-08-25,
       by request): the extra x buys the obstacle field room to sit clear
       of both the corridor and the arm base. Everything else is the lab's
       measured cell. ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop_real/ keeps the true 0.763.

       The world frame is now the ROBOT BASE frame as well (base at the
       origin, `world_frame="xarm_device"` in oim/utils/scenes.py), so
       FoundationPose's TF reads straight into the planner with no
       world -> base transform. Every coordinate below is in that frame.

       Keep in sync with the four `_tee_scene` entries in
       oim/utils/scenes.py -- the planner's analytic object model is built
       from those numbers, and nothing checks the two agree at runtime
       except tests/test_scenes.py. -->

  <worldbody>
    <!-- THE LAB TABLE, identical to ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop_real/common_real.xml's
         (see there for the measurement and for why it is one solid slab),
         widened from 763 to 800 mm in x. MJCF `size` is half-extents, so the
         table spans x in [-0.05, 0.75] and y in [-0.7615, 0.7615], top
         surface at z = 0. The near edge stays at x = -0.05 -- the arm is
         clamped to it -- so the extra depth goes forward, away from the arm.

         The arm is clamped at the middle of the rear long edge and sits
         0.05 m in from it, which is what puts the near edge at x = -0.05.

         contype/conaffinity 3 so this collides with both the arm (1) and the
         floor plane (2) -- and with the block, whose geoms carry 3 too. The
         `<contact>` pairs at the bottom of this file are what actually give
         the block its planar friction. -->
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.40 0.7615 0.455" pos="0.35 0 -0.455"
      contype="3" conaffinity="3" rgba="0.95 0.95 0.95 1"/>

    <!-- Mirrors the block's geoms exactly, at the goal pose, so an aligned
         block occupies precisely the marker's volume -- overlap is the
         success criterion by eye, and the stem makes the 180-degree
         ambiguity of a bare crossbar impossible to misread.

         The goal keeps IsaacGym's own 180-degree flip about z from the
         block's spawn orientation (MJCF quat is wxyz, hence "0 0 0 1"); only
         its position moved. The corridor now runs start (0.381, +0.400) ->
         goal (0.381, -0.400), a 0.800 m push straight down the table's long
         axis, with the obstacle field confined to |y| <= 0.3 -- so both ends
         of every task start at least 0.1 m clear of anything in the way. -->
    <body name="goal" mocap="true" pos="0.381 -0.400 0.0298" quat="0 0 0 1">
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_crossbar" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0"/>
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_stem" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Ghost of the pushed object at the object block's horizon endpoint
         x^{o*}_H, moved every control step by the runners (mocap, so it is
         written straight into mocap_pos/mocap_quat and never simulated).
         Geoms mirror `block`'s exactly, at the same z, so its overlap with
         the block reads the same way the goal marker's does. `pos`/`quat`
         here are only the pre-first-plan placeholder; oim/runtime/mjcf.py
         parks it on the block's start pose. -->
    <body name="local_goal" mocap="true" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0298" quat="1 0 0 0">
      <geom class="local_goal" name="local_goal_crossbar" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0"/>
      <geom class="local_goal" name="local_goal_stem" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0"/>
    </body>

    <!-- THE MEASURED LAB T-BLOCK, verbatim from ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop_real/tee_real.xml:
         89 x 99 mm in plan, 59.6 mm thick, 0.1 kg. It replaces IsaacGym's
         tee_block.urdf (0.200 x 0.150 m, 2.0 kg), which was 2.2x larger in
         plan than anything the lab actually pushes.

         The body anchor stays at (0, 0): `PushT._block_pose` reads `qpos`,
         the T_x/T_y joints' displacement from this anchor, and treats it as
         the world pose. The start pose lives in common.xml's "start"
         keyframe instead (and in `object_start` on the scene spec).

         Body z is 0.0298, the geoms' own half-height, so the bottom face
         lands exactly on the table top at z = 0 and the block starts
         resting -- `tip_target_z` reads this z as the block's mid-height,
         so a rounded value would put every tip-height cost off the block it
         is measured against.

         The joints carry NO frictionloss: support friction is the table
         contact's, mu*N, so it responds to how hard the block is pressed
         (see the <contact> block below). -->
    <body name="block" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0298">
      <joint name="T_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_z" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <!-- Vertical DoF, so the block falls when it leaves the table instead
           of hovering, and so it has a normal force at all. Declared after
           T_x/T_y/T_z so the SE(2) pose stays at the first three qpos
           entries, which oim/tasks/pusht.py reads positionally.

           z only, no roll/pitch: with tipping DoFs the block's contact
           blows up in the robot block's Warp rollouts at planning_dt=0.05
           (NaN states within ~3 control steps, damping only delays it). The
           block therefore stays level and drops once fully clear of the
           edge rather than tipping over it. -->
      <joint name="T_zs" type="slide" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <geom name="block_crossbar" contype="3" conaffinity="3" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0" mass="0.05" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
      <geom name="block_stem" contype="3" conaffinity="3" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0" mass="0.05" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- The block's planar resistance is the table CONTACT's, not a constant
       `frictionloss` bound on its joints: condim="3" with mu = 0.3, the
       lab's own measured object/table coefficient and the same mu the
       analytic limit surface assumes, so friction is mu*N and rises when the
       pusher presses down.

       Why it is not `frictionloss`: that is a fixed force bound, so pressing
       on the block costs nothing and the simulator cannot represent the one
       thing that makes top-riding expensive. Measured breakaway in +x
       against a downward press, on the simulated block --

           press          0 N     5 N    10 N    20 N
           frictionloss   8.00    8.00    8.00    8.00   <- free
           contact mu     8.00   10.00   12.00   16.00   <- mu*(mg+F)

       At zero press the two agree, which is what keeps mu*m*g = 0.2943 N
       meaningful as the NOMINAL budget that
       `PlanarPushingObject.wrench_limit` and every ADMM normalization read.
       `solimp` stays stiff because a soft normal constraint lets the block
       sink into the table under a press, silently reducing N.

       The torque channel is an OUTCOME of the footprint's geometry, not a
       number declared here, so `limit_surface_radius` in oim/utils/scenes.py
       is measured against this block rather than chosen. -->
  <contact>
    <pair geom1="block_crossbar" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
    <pair geom1="block_stem" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
  </contact>

  <sensor>
    <framepos name="position" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
    <framequat name="orientation" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
  </sensor>

  <statistic center="0.381 0 0" extent="0.9"/>
</mujoco>
